    <item>
      <title>Crowds and Power or Why Everyone at Your Office Acts Weird in Meetings</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/crowds/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 28 Mar 2025 12:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/crowds/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;figure&gt;&#xA;    &lt;picture&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/webp&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/crowds_hu_5f034a0db2df6741.webp 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/crowds_hu_1f35b67dcfab9e5.webp 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/crowds_hu_55c08d8c9b8156a1.webp 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/jpeg&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/crowds_hu_d2a68618e12f415d.jpg 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/crowds_hu_e987d8f664209732.jpg 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/crowds_hu_876aea591665ed43.jpg 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;img&#xA;        src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/crowds_hu_e987d8f664209732.jpg&#34;&#xA;        alt=&#34;image of a vast crowd&#34;&#xA;        class=&#34;&#34;&#xA;        width=&#34;800&#34;&#xA;        height=&#34;400&#34;&#xA;        loading=&#34;lazy&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;&lt;/picture&gt; &#xA;    &lt;figcaption&gt;crowds&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&#xA;So here&amp;rsquo;s a fun thing about human nature, Elias Canetti spent thirty-five years &lt;em&gt;thirty-five years!&lt;/em&gt; writing a book about why people in groups act completely insane. This seems like a long time to think about crowd behavior, but then again, Canetti lived through both World Wars, saw the rise of Hitler, Stalin, and Mussolini, and watched millions of seemingly rational individuals turn into howling mobs. So, you know, he had some material to work with.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Pierre Bayle&#39;s Dictionary or How to Subtweet the Entire 17th Century</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/critical-dictionary/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 28 Feb 2025 12:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/critical-dictionary/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;figure&gt;&#xA;    &lt;picture&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/webp&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/fortune-teller_hu_ff075d7177c3d284.webp 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/fortune-teller_hu_7533a54ba40630ba.webp 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/fortune-teller_hu_7d8e464182f960cd.webp 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/jpeg&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/fortune-teller_hu_7929b781e4fa8420.jpg 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/fortune-teller_hu_44a39e40cce674bf.jpg 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/fortune-teller_hu_ada740a132b303c.jpg 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;img&#xA;        src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/fortune-teller_hu_44a39e40cce674bf.jpg&#34;&#xA;        alt=&#34;&#34;&#xA;        class=&#34;&#34;&#xA;        width=&#34;800&#34;&#xA;        height=&#34;661&#34;&#xA;        loading=&#34;lazy&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;&lt;/picture&gt; &#xA;    &lt;figcaption&gt;A fortune teller reading cards&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If you were a Protestant intellectual in 1690s France, you had basically three career options: (1) convert to Catholicism, (2) flee to Holland, or (3) get thrown in prison. Pierre Bayle went with option two, which turned out great for the history of Western thought because what he did next was absolutely bonkers. He wrote a dictionary.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Augustine&#39;s Confessions or The First Guy to Write About His Feelings on the Internet</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/confessions/</link>
      <pubDate>Sat, 28 Dec 2024 12:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/confessions/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-deal&#34;&gt;The Deal&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So around 397 AD, this guy Augustine who is basically running the Catholic Church&amp;rsquo;s North Africa division from his office in Hippo&lt;sup id=&#34;fnref:1&#34;&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#fn:1&#34; class=&#34;footnote-ref&#34; role=&#34;doc-noteref&#34;&gt;1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;—decides to write a book about all the terrible things he did before he became a bishop. This is weird! Imagine if Jamie Dimon wrote a 13-volume memoir that started with &amp;ldquo;Let me tell you about the time I stole some pears when I was sixteen, and boy, do I have &lt;em&gt;thoughts&lt;/em&gt; about it.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Democracy in America</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/democracy-america/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 28 Oct 2024 12:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/democracy-america/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;democracy-in-america-is-weird&#34;&gt;Democracy in America Is Weird&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;figure&gt;&#xA;    &lt;picture&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/webp&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/sign_hu_7d7f73842b470d92.webp 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/sign_hu_c2a38f7f4fbb5b11.webp 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/sign_hu_3d9e7ad49ba7333.webp 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/jpeg&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/sign_hu_265192f4929ec7fa.jpg 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/sign_hu_3aab832bdc50c377.jpg 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/sign_hu_454a4b8c109a8cb5.jpg 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;img&#xA;        src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/sign_hu_3aab832bdc50c377.jpg&#34;&#xA;        alt=&#34;scene at the signing of the constitution of America&#34;&#xA;        class=&#34;&#34;&#xA;        width=&#34;800&#34;&#xA;        height=&#34;515&#34;&#xA;        loading=&#34;lazy&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;&lt;/picture&gt; &#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So there was this French guy, Alexis de Tocqueville, who showed up in America in 1831, supposedly to look at prisons but really to figure out what the deal was with democracy. And look, I know what you&amp;rsquo;re thinking: &amp;ldquo;Why do I care what some 19th-century French aristocrat thought about America?&amp;rdquo; But here&amp;rsquo;s the thing, the guy basically invented political science Twitter 200 years early.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Machiavelli&#39;s Other Book Is About Republics Actually</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/discourses-on-livy/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 28 Aug 2024 12:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/discourses-on-livy/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;figure&gt;&#xA;    &lt;picture&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/webp&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/mach_hu_f270cbdbb26a2c47.webp 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/mach_hu_123ec754a53d6360.webp 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/mach_hu_f390de7f39131c50.webp 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/jpeg&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/mach_hu_a3d08da120e1a446.jpg 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/mach_hu_404cc7e9c29ff765.jpg 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/mach_hu_8e88ae4ace028879.jpg 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;img&#xA;        src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/mach_hu_404cc7e9c29ff765.jpg&#34;&#xA;        alt=&#34;&#34;&#xA;        class=&#34;&#34;&#xA;        width=&#34;800&#34;&#xA;        height=&#34;460&#34;&#xA;        loading=&#34;lazy&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;&lt;/picture&gt; &#xA;    &lt;figcaption&gt;Machiavelli and his domain&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-context-thing&#34;&gt;The Context Thing&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So here&amp;rsquo;s a fun story about Niccolò Machiavelli. Everyone knows him as the &amp;ldquo;ends justify the means&amp;rdquo; guy who wrote &lt;em&gt;The Prince&lt;/em&gt;, that cynical handbook about how to be a ruthless dictator. But here&amp;rsquo;s the thing: Machiavelli actually preferred republics! I know! The guy whose name literally became an adjective for scheming political manipulation spent most of his intellectual energy writing a massive love letter to republican government. It&amp;rsquo;s like finding out that Gordon Gekko&amp;rsquo;s favorite book was actually about credit unions.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Hobbes&#39;s Leviathan or How to Not Murder Each Other Over Politics</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/leviathan/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 28 Jun 2024 12:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/leviathan/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-context-or-why-everyone-was-so-upset&#34;&gt;The Context, or Why Everyone Was So Upset&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So it&amp;rsquo;s 1651 and Thomas Hobbes has just watched England tear itself apart for like a decade.The English Civil War (1642-1651)    &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.britannica.com/event/English-Civil-Wars&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&#xA; was basically what happens when you take the question &amp;ldquo;who should be in charge?&amp;rdquo; and answer it with &amp;ldquo;let&amp;rsquo;s find out with swords.&amp;rdquo; King Charles I thought God wanted him to run everything. Parliament thought, no, actually, we should have a say. The Puritans thought everyone else was doing Christianity wrong. The Catholics were nervous. The Scots were involved somehow. It was a mess.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>My thinking for the 2025</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/my-thinking/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 28 Jun 2024 12:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/my-thinking/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;I don’t know where to start, but lets say this is not something I need to write but I think circumstances asked for it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;figure&gt;&#xA;    &lt;picture&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/webp&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/thinking-2025_hu_b2c00f12c522326b.webp 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/thinking-2025_hu_9db7063b0f6d1659.webp 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/thinking-2025_hu_154aa2e2679aee88.webp 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/jpeg&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/thinking-2025_hu_e99153ee3b4914a0.jpg 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/thinking-2025_hu_89351d77fa827160.jpg 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/thinking-2025_hu_78ced4ab1cef1342.jpg 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;img&#xA;        src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/thinking-2025_hu_89351d77fa827160.jpg&#34;&#xA;        alt=&#34;this is how this year feels like&#34;&#xA;        class=&#34;&#34;&#xA;        width=&#34;800&#34;&#xA;        height=&#34;605&#34;&#xA;        loading=&#34;lazy&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;&lt;/picture&gt; &#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;iframe&#xA;  src=&#34;https://open.spotify.com/embed/album/71nSsMDGcGFwHqknIELTe8&#34;&#xA;  width=&#34;300&#34;&#xA;  height=&#34;380&#34;&#xA;  frameborder=&#34;0&#34;&#xA;  allowtransparency=&#34;true&#34;&#xA;  allow=&#34;autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; fullscreen; picture-in-picture&#34;&#xA;  loading=&#34;lazy&#34;&#xA;&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and this is how a github gist looks like&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Hello! I have read your notes, and I have to say: &lt;strong&gt;you have excellent intuition for how Git works.&lt;/strong&gt; You have correctly identified the core logic (the &amp;ldquo;happy path&amp;rdquo;) of saving work.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Australia Is Running a Weird Experiment in Coasting</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/lucky-country/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 10 May 2024 12:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/lucky-country/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-lucky-country-thing&#34;&gt;The Lucky Country Thing&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So there&amp;rsquo;s this thing about Australia. In 1964, a journalist named Donald Horne wrote a book called &amp;ldquo;The Lucky Country&lt;sup id=&#34;fnref:1&#34;&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#fn:1&#34; class=&#34;footnote-ref&#34; role=&#34;doc-noteref&#34;&gt;1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;rdquo; and here&amp;rsquo;s the funny part, everyone thinks it&amp;rsquo;s a compliment. It&amp;rsquo;s not!. The whole point was that Australia was lucky, not good. Lucky is what you are when you&amp;rsquo;re not particularly competent but things work out anyway. It&amp;rsquo;s like being a hedge fund manager in a bull market.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Ovid&#39;s Metamorphoses or How to Get Exiled for Writing Fanfiction</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/metamorphasis-ovid/</link>
      <pubDate>Sun, 28 Apr 2024 12:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/metamorphasis-ovid/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Look, so around 8 CE, Publius Ovidius Naso, Ovid to his friends, probably &amp;ldquo;that guy who won&amp;rsquo;t shut up about Zeus turning into animals&amp;rdquo; to everyone else finished writing what is essentially the world&amp;rsquo;s most ambitious mythological cinematic universe. The &lt;em&gt;Metamorphoses&lt;/em&gt; is 15 books of people turning into things, gods behaving badly, and what happens when you give shapeshifting powers to entities with impulse control problems.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;figure&gt;&#xA;    &lt;picture&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/webp&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/ovid_hu_e985115385346ef4.webp 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/ovid_hu_485ac3cc0118d36b.webp 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/ovid_hu_d519ad9a4025fad5.webp 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/jpeg&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/ovid_hu_20b3ead6d4dfd282.jpg 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/ovid_hu_a1e2b8e24af84e53.jpg 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/ovid_hu_c7a91b82c7bcd8fd.jpg 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;img&#xA;        src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/ovid_hu_a1e2b8e24af84e53.jpg&#34;&#xA;        alt=&#34;&#34;&#xA;        class=&#34;&#34;&#xA;        width=&#34;800&#34;&#xA;        height=&#34;557&#34;&#xA;        loading=&#34;lazy&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;&lt;/picture&gt; &#xA;    &lt;figcaption&gt;Ovid side profile&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title> Aristotle&#39;s Nicomachean Ethics or How to Not Be a Jerk</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/nicomachean-ethics/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 28 Mar 2024 12:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/nicomachean-ethics/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;figure&gt;&#xA;    &lt;picture&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/webp&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/zero_hu_a75957d765e54b8c.webp 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/zero_hu_b82b6302d9bd4490.webp 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/zero_hu_3ecfb9e82a45a0eb.webp 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/jpeg&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/zero_hu_a1f0d1c5f5a62775.jpg 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/zero_hu_3bb9351d47791857.jpg 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/zero_hu_14dfc8f860daff81.jpg 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;img&#xA;        src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/zero_hu_3bb9351d47791857.jpg&#34;&#xA;        alt=&#34;&#34;&#xA;        class=&#34;&#34;&#xA;        width=&#34;800&#34;&#xA;        height=&#34;471&#34;&#xA;        loading=&#34;lazy&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;&lt;/picture&gt; &#xA;    &lt;figcaption&gt;Ethics or what?&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;programming-note&#34;&gt;Programming Note&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If you&amp;rsquo;re reading this expecting tips on tax-efficient charitable giving strategies, I have bad news. Nicomachean Ethics is not about the tax code. It&amp;rsquo;s about how to live a good life, which, depending on your perspective, might be either more or less complicated than the tax code.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Progress and Poverty or Why Does Everything Get Worse When It Gets Better?</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/progress-and-poverty/</link>
      <pubDate>Sat, 27 Jan 2024 12:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/exp/posts/progress-and-poverty/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-rent-is-too-damn-high-1879-edition&#34;&gt;    &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OUx_32ABtw4&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;&gt;The Rent Is Too Damn High&lt;/a&gt;&#xA; (1879 Edition)&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;figure&gt;&#xA;    &lt;picture&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/webp&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/robber-barons_hu_a5a55c14ee824a1b.webp 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/robber-barons_hu_970b6a79cee914c4.webp 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/robber-barons_hu_9e190a851a3ed50d.webp 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;source&#xA;        type=&#34;image/jpeg&#34;&#xA;        srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/robber-barons_hu_4b9af52f9e9b6af3.jpg 480w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/robber-barons_hu_c8023afb0a51770a.jpg 800w, http://localhost:1313/exp/images/robber-barons_hu_4e1f5cc41485db03.jpg 1200w&#34;&#xA;        sizes=&#34;(max-width: 800px) 90vw, 800px&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;    &lt;img&#xA;        src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/exp/images/robber-barons_hu_c8023afb0a51770a.jpg&#34;&#xA;        alt=&#34;Robber barrons eating the world or America at the time&#34;&#xA;        class=&#34;&#34;&#xA;        width=&#34;800&#34;&#xA;        height=&#34;537&#34;&#xA;        loading=&#34;lazy&#34;&#xA;    /&gt;&#xA;&lt;/picture&gt; &#xA;    &lt;figcaption&gt;Robber barrons eating the world or America at the time&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So here&amp;rsquo;s a weird thing that happened in 1879. A guy named Henry George looked around San Francisco, hich was having a absolutely bonkers real estate boom thanks to railroads and gold and general Western expansion vibes and asked a question that sounds extremely familiar: Why is it that the more prosperous society gets, the more impossible it becomes for regular people to afford anything?&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
